WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.160
OK, so welcome to
another week of AA203.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.860
So starting from our usual
roadmap, in the last week or so,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:21.200
we have started-- basically,
we finished the optimal control

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.525
part of the class, and we
moved into the top half, which

00:00:24.525 --> 00:00:26.400
basically relates with
learning-based methods

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.320
for optimal control.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.780
Last week we focused
a lot on-- well,

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.200
on Monday we focused on
more classical methods

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.340
to learning based control.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.600
So we looked at things such
as system identification

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.160
and adaptive control.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.080
And then on last
week, Wednesday, we

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.400
started to introduce
the general framework

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.320
and terminology for imitation
learning and reinforcement

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.880
learning and starting to
build a bit of a mental model

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.720
about how to see different
methods and different algorithms

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.040
within these two subclasses.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.120
And so, specifically,
today, we'll

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.410
then zoom into the
specifically imitation learning

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:05.209
section of what we
discussed on Wednesday.

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:06.930
And so if you
remember, we basically

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:12.650
introduced imitation learning as
basically an umbrella term that

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.670
encompasses, broadly speaking,
two main classes of approaches.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.890
We started from behavior cloning
as essentially a mechanism where

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.310
we want to have access.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.010
So we always assume
we have access

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.990
to a data set of couples
of states and controls,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.530
which we collected by
observing some expert executing

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.130
some demonstrations in the
real world, for example.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.010
And then in behavior
cloning, we want

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.570
to use this data set to
directly learn a policy

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.970
or approximate the policy that
is being used by the expert.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.490
Concretely, the object
that we wish to learn

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.810
will be something along the
lines of policy pi of theta.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.780
For example, a
parameterized policy

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.940
with some parameters of
theta that is essentially

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.840
representing a distribution
over controls given states.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.100
And we want to approximate
this from the examples

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.420
that we get in our
demonstrations.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.840
We then also loosely introduced
inverse reinforcement learning,

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.780
whereby in this
case, the objective

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.220
is to approximate the reward
function or objective that

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.380
is optimized that we think it is
being optimized under the hood

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.060
by the expert itself.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.500
And specifically, here
the object of learning

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.760
will be to approximate or
estimate our reward function, r,

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.860
that is basically
a measure that maps

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.920
couples of states and controls
to a scalar measure of success.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.020
And so, for today,
a kind of coherent

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.260
with this general roadmap
will be discussing

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.680
behavior cloning first.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.020
We'll be introducing a number
of common pitfalls, which

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.840
we already slightly hinted to in
our last lecture on Wednesday.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.180
And specifically, I
would say starting

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:57.660
to look at
traditional mechanisms

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.580
for which these pitfalls
are being addressed.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.980
And then we'll have a brief
mention on other paradigms

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.740
that fall within the
behavioral cloning hat.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.820
And then we'll look at
inverse enforcement learning.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.700
So just to briefly
recap in one slide

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.500
the lecture from
Wednesday, we started

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.340
by seeing behavioral
cloning essentially

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.560
as supervised
learning of behavior.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.740
So we introduced
the general notation

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.860
of supervised learning,
whereby we essentially

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:28.020
want to learn a parametric
mapping that maps some inputs,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.260
x, to some outputs, y.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.620
Prototypical example would
be the classification problem

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.740
where we want to map some
image representation,

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.380
for example, an RGB
image, an RGB that

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.940
represents the image
of a cat to its label

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.420
with of the respective object
or thing that is actually being

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.300
represented in that image.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.860
And the way to do
this is by taking

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.710
a data set of pairs
of this mapping

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.870
that we want to estimate
and then learn it

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.670
through some sort of
loss minimization.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.590
In behavioral cloning, we want
to do something very similar,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.110
to some extent, because we
want to collect a data set

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.830
of these expert demonstrations.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.570
And exactly as we would be
doing in supervised learning,

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.070
we would want to
train a policy that

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:18.029
is this mapping that optimizes
some metric of performance.

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:21.589
And so we saw how the general
skeleton of behavior cloning

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:23.590
algorithm, loosely
speaking, really looks

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.930
like something like
supervised learning

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.750
and now apply it to trying
to estimate behavior.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.810
But the question here
is, is this really it?

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:34.650
Does this work?

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.530
And are we done?

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.430
And, in practice, as you can
imagine, most of the times this

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.228
is not it, in the
sense that there

00:04:40.228 --> 00:04:42.270
are a number of common
pitfalls and failure modes

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.290
that could happen.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.670
And so today we'll basically
be looking at strategies

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.490
to address these failure modes.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.590
And I would say look
at different techniques

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.160
that frontier your approaches,
specifically in learning based

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.600
control and robotics
are currently doing

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.020
to make these techniques work.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.640
And so let us start from
introducing the common pitfalls

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.800
that could emerge.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.680
And the first one is one
of compounding errors.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.320
Specifically, we
mentioned multiple times

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.480
how in statistical
learning theory

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.200
a classical assumption
that is made

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.080
is that data is Independent and
Identically Distributed, or IID,

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.080
for short.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.080
Loosely speaking,
this means that we

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.920
are taking some
distribution of p of x,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.880
which we wish to approximate.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.200
To do this, we only have access
to samples or realizations

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.900
of that distributions, x1
to x3 here on the slide.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.800
And we want to learn typically
a parametric model that

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.280
approximates this p of x
that we care in learning.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.560
And we saw that, again,
generally speaking,

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.200
in statistical
learning theory, we

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.210
assume that the samples and
realization that we observe

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.390
are IID from the distribution
that we care about.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.750
When trying to learn
behaviors, however,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.850
kind of predictive controls
influence the future state

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.250
that we observe.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.450
And we loosely and briefly
discussed this idea of the fact

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:09.190
that since we are agents
within our environment,

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:12.430
there could be this problem and
issue of compounding errors,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.990
whereby if our policy is
making even very small errors

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:21.210
it could compound over time that
is throughout the temporal kind

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.690
of unrolling of our trajectory,
which lead to making bigger

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:28.130
and bigger errors over time.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:32.410
Slightly more formally, theory
here formalizes this problem

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.650
as the fact that the
state distribution induced

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:39.130
by the expert policy and the
state distribution induced

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.810
by the learner, that
is, the policy that we

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:45.250
wish to learn essentially
diverge and are not the same.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.900
And this is, in theory, referred
to as the covariate shift

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.380
problem.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.420
So to make the notation on
the slide more concrete, when

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:58.180
we say the state distribution
under the expert, that

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:03.260
is p of expert of x, what
we're essentially saying

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:08.020
is, for example, the
probability of the initial state

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:11.780
times the product, p
that goes from 1 to t

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.860
of the expert policy--

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.720
so the probability of the action
induced by the expert policy,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.540
given the state xt
times the transition

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.760
function of our environment.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.780
So this is basically encodes
the probability distribution

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.020
of all the states
that we could observe

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.940
by following the policy
or the distribution

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:49.110
over actions induced
by the expert.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:55.350
Very similarly, the state
distribution under our policy,

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:58.790
p of pi theta,
would essentially be

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:02.430
designed defined exactly the
same where here we just used

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.710
the distribution over
controls induced by the policy

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.890
that we're currently learning.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:15.727
And there is--

00:08:15.727 --> 00:08:18.310
I would definitely advise you
to check out this reference here

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:21.630
on the slide because there is
theory that basically shows

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:28.350
that the probability of making
mistakes under our policy

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.870
essentially grows
quadratically with the length

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.190
of the trajectory.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.750
So here assuming that there
is a probability epsilon

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:39.470
to make a mistake in our
prediction from our policy,

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:42.350
then the probability
of making mistakes

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:47.310
grows quadratically with the
length, t, of our trajectory.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.510
Of course, here
the error assumes

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.430
that we are working
in a discrete world

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:55.130
where we have to pick between
1 of n possible actions

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:57.070
and epsilon is the
probability of making

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.610
an error in that prediction.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:00.110
But there is a
lot of theory that

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.830
goes about formulating
this covariate shift

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.430
problem more theoretically.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.670
But I hope the
intuition here is clear.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.594
Yes.

00:09:09.594 --> 00:09:10.578
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.310
The trajectory itself
are independent, yes.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:20.230
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:23.970
This is a good question.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.990
So, in general, for
now, and I would

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:27.948
say throughout the
rest of this lecture,

00:09:27.948 --> 00:09:29.990
we're assuming that you're
interested in learning

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.410
the feedback policy.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:36.110
So there is a mapping from
the instantaneous state, xt,

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.670
to the instantaneous
control, ut.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.110
And our data set,
therefore, is just

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.620
a collection of two poles
of this realization.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.700
And so this is for the
compounding error problem.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.440
A second problem is the
one that here I refer

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.480
to as multimodal behavior.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:09.120
And specifically,
in many tasks, there

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.520
is this characteristic
whereby there

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.240
could exist multiple
and equivalent solutions

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.100
to solving a control task.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.680
So take the pictorial example
of flying a drone past

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.840
a tree here on the slide.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.720
If we were to be
human experts or human

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.120
that are kind of joysticking
this drone around,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.400
we would be in a situation
where flying to the right

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.680
or to the left of the
tree would be equivalently

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.800
good to some extent.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.320
And the problem
here arises when we

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.090
want to take this data set
of collected demonstrations

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:46.330
from, for example,
human rollouts

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:50.370
and use these data sets
to compile or learn

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:54.850
a policy that tries to
approximate this behavior.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.650
Slightly more
formally, the problem

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.370
here is that we find ourselves
in our situation, whereby

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.250
the policy that we
wish to estimate

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:06.210
is actually representing
a multi-modal distribution

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:09.810
over controls given states.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.330
For example, like
in this case, we

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.450
might want our
policy pi of theta

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:18.810
to perfectly approximate this
bimodal distribution over here.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.570
And the problem here
emerges in the moment

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:24.570
where we, for example, try
to estimate things or fit

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:27.550
this distribution through
pretty standard metrics,

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.170
for example, mean squared
error or minimization.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:34.010
And the point is that if we were
to take, say, a neural network

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:37.190
and try to fit our data through
mean squared error minimization,

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.540
we would be essentially
learning a policy that

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:44.740
tries to predict exactly the
mean of our distributions.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:49.300
So what we're saying
is that we are

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:53.340
in a situation where we would
like to approximate ground truth

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:57.400
behavior distribution that
looks something like this.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.380
That is my model.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.300
But if we were to use
mean squared error

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.340
as our loss for model
for data fitting,

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.500
our policy would
then be incentivized

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:11.060
to learn something
that correctly

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:14.145
predicts the mean of this
multimodal distribution.

00:12:14.145 --> 00:12:16.020
And you see how in this
case this is actually

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:19.060
probably the worst thing that we
could do because it fits exactly

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.020
in the middle because
it tries to balance out

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:25.957
errors between both modes.

00:12:25.957 --> 00:12:27.540
And this is actually
a problem that is

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.140
extremely common in practice.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.700
For example, just consider
where we are collecting data

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.700
from multiple experts which
might behave differently

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.500
in the same condition.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:41.830
And so this is something that
we will have to take care of.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:42.770
Does this make sense?

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.130
Any questions on this?

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.990
So for the rest of
today, we will basically

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.750
be building what I'm calling
here a useful mental model

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.710
for basically developing
techniques that

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:02.670
are remedies for these issues.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.630
Specifically, we'll be looking
at algorithms which basically

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.130
try to address these
problems specifically,

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:11.510
or at least from an
algorithmic standpoint.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.150
We'll be looking
at ways in which we

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:16.710
could address these problems
from a data collection

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:17.670
perspective.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:20.870
And then we'll be seeing how
we can use expressive model

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:24.430
classes to better represent
the distribution of our data.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.430
And so, specifically,
the first two bullet

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.830
points here on the slide that
is targeted algorithms and data

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:34.910
collection will be focusing
on the compounding errors

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.350
kind of issue, while
the third one will

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:41.090
focus on the multimodality
of our policy in our data.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.370
OK, so let us start
from the first one.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.790
So algorithms that try
to specifically devise

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.390
methods to target these issues.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:58.270
And very important aspect
which I want to highlight

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:04.390
is essentially the value that
there is in corrective data.

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:06.830
So until now, we're basically
discussing a situation

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:09.550
where we are collecting
data from an expert, which

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:12.590
and we always kind of
hinted at this fact

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.470
where we basically
want to compile

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.330
the performance of our
expert into a learned model,

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:19.750
a parametric function.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:23.750
And we always said that the
performance of our model

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:29.590
is at least at the ideally as
good as the quality in our data.

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:34.220
And so it probably came
as a following thought.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.600
The fact is that we want to
keep our data the best possible,

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.680
so we want to collect
demonstrations

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:43.360
that are exceptionally
good in solving the task.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:45.600
The problem is that if
we do so, most likely

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.080
we'll be ending up
with a data set that

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:52.200
is extremely narrow around
a specific kind of behavior

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.640
in solving that task.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:15:04.120
And if you wish, this leads to
kind of a counterintuitive fact.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:09.160
Because, in general, if we
want to apply behavior cloning

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:12.880
to our learning-based
control problem,

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:18.440
there is a lot of value in
leveraging examples of errors

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:20.360
that have been taken
by the experts and ways

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:23.720
to recover from those errors
because in practice, whenever we

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:26.500
deploy our policies
in the real world,

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.400
the policy will be
making some errors.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:31.650
And so if we have an extremely
perfect data with no errors

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.850
whatsoever, in those states
where the policy actually

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.770
makes errors, it will
have no clue of how

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:38.930
to recover from those errors.

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:40.930
And so there is
this paradox whereby

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:43.410
learning from a broader
set of trajectories that

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.690
makes some mistakes
but also shows

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.930
ways to correct
from these mistakes

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:51.330
is, in general, more valuable
than learning from a narrow set

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:52.790
of perfect demonstrations.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:03.370
And one way to automate
this labeling and collection

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:09.690
of corrective data is through an
algorithm is known as a DAgger.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:13.330
And specifically, DAgger
is a well-known method

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:15.770
to address this covariate
shift problem through what

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:18.250
are called data aggregation
techniques, which is also

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:21.130
where the name comes from.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:24.090
And I think it's
probably useful to go

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:30.020
through a pseudocode of how
at least the plain version

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:33.740
of DAgger looks like and then we
discussed this a little bit more

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.100
in detail.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.700
And specifically, in DAgger, we
start from an initial data set

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:40.778
of expert demonstrations,
things that we

00:16:40.778 --> 00:16:42.820
have collected by running
some policy in the wild

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:44.820
or observing some human.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:49.700
We will have an initial
policy, pi theta superscript 1,

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:51.580
where here the
superscript refers

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:55.440
to the number of iterations
of the DAgger algorithm.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.940
So DAgger will be an iterative
algorithm where we are iterating

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.920
from 1 to n iterations.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.900
And so here the subscript
refers to the policy

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.220
that is derived at iteration i.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:13.780
So what DAgger does is
we start our iterations.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:15.900
And for each of
these iterations we

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.720
will be collecting
some trajectories, tau,

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:23.619
which basically represent the
couples of states and controls

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.500
which we observe by
letting the learner policy

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:28.490
interact with our environment.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:29.930
So we have a given policy.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:31.950
We let it interact
with the environment,

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.090
and we collect data
from these rollouts.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.710
What we want to
do is then we want

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:41.590
to take these
states that we have

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:43.950
visited during our rollouts.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:46.710
And in these states, we
want to query our experts

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:48.070
for relabeling.

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:51.070
That is, we want to take
those same states, xt.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:54.230
And for those states, we want
to relabel the control that

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:58.150
was originally taken from
the learner with the control

00:17:58.150 --> 00:17:59.410
from the expert.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:04.350
Once this is done,
we have a new version

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.270
of our data set,
the superscript i,

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:09.930
and we aggregate it
with the original data

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:12.830
set which we had for learning.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:15.670
And so this is where the
aggregation part comes from.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.830
We then take this
aggregated data set, d,

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:22.110
and use this to train a
new version of our policy.

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:24.230
So maybe slightly
more visually, we

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:29.240
are saying that we have
a policy, pi of theta 1

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:30.220
given to us.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:33.680
This could have been given to us
by an offline data set that we

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:35.920
have and trained on this.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.600
We then have our environment,
which might look like something

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.920
where we want to go from
some starting points

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:46.440
to some goal points, where
we are seeing examples

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:50.240
of rollouts of our policy.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:59.440
And this leads to a data
set of states and controls

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:03.880
where the controls are the
ones that the learner executed.

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.760
So we can then use this data
set to basically relabel

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:11.220
those controls with controls
given by the expert.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:16.840
This creates a new
data set, d superscript

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:19.720
i, which we aggregated
to the previous one

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.040
and train on the
updated data set.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.920
So why is this useful?

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:29.360
The point here is
that by doing so,

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:35.520
we are directly querying the
expert to relabel states exactly

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.800
in those states that
are currently being

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.380
visited by the learner itself.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.560
So we were talking
about this discrepancy

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.880
between the
distribution of states

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:49.880
induced by the learner and
the one induced by the expert.

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:53.400
In this case, we are
explicitly querying the expert

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:57.280
in those states that
would be followed

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.120
and are essentially drawn
from the state distribution

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.720
under the learner.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.480
Yeah.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.280
Are you just relabeling?

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.880
There as a successful control
and then relabeling it

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.920
as an expert and retraining
that now that [INAUDIBLE] expert

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:18.920
or training it from
your original expert?

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.640
So here we are assuming we
have access to an expert

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:22.870
that we can query.

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:25.210
This could be any policy
that we think is good enough

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.770
for us to be an expert.

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:30.210
And we are using this
to basically generate

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:33.650
new labeled data
for us in some way.

00:20:33.650 --> 00:20:35.770
Does it answer your question?

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:38.030
Yeah.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:43.410
In practice, if the source of
the expert policy is a human,

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.790
is this a discrete process where
you would have to step through?

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:48.830
Because if you want
the exact same states,

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:52.510
you have to step through and
not continuously provide input

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:54.890
but go state to state
to state and provide

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.430
a discrete input and then that
is the relabeling process?

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:58.847
It's a great
question because this

00:20:58.847 --> 00:21:00.810
is if you wish one version--

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:03.770
this is like you
would see DAgger v0.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.430
So this is the base
version of DAgger.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:06.930
There is an extension
to this, which

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:10.130
is referred to as human
gated DAgger, whereby

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:13.030
which will be in the next slide,
which we're basically here,

00:21:13.030 --> 00:21:16.410
we would be picking a specific
point in our trajectory

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:19.210
where we think maybe the
learner made a mistake.

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:22.420
And here we assume we have a
human that basically intervenes

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:27.100
and generates a new, full
continuation of that trajectory.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:31.540
Concretely, you have a robot
arm that is doing some task.

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:35.240
You're physically looking at
the arm while it's doing things,

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:37.180
but here you're
seeing that maybe it's

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:38.760
spilled over something.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:39.857
And so you're taking over.

00:21:39.857 --> 00:21:41.940
And from there, you're
maybe teleoperating the arm

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:43.320
to generate the new trajectory.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:46.700
And in that case, you would
be limiting the augmented data

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:50.040
set to have the exact same
states as the original one.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.780
You would allow yourself
to explore a different part

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:54.820
after the mistake?

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:55.660
Yeah.

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.482
Which, in a way,
this is a nice segue,

00:21:58.482 --> 00:21:59.940
because if you
think about it, this

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.440
is only one iteration
of the algorithm.

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:05.220
But if you repeat
this, then you're

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.380
basically you will
be having rollouts

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:12.120
of a new policy, pi 2, which has
a different state distribution.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:16.060
And so here you will be
covering new set of states.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:18.710
So very high level.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:22.050
DAgger is an automated way
to take your given policy.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:24.790
You let it interact
in your environment,

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:28.030
and you basically expose
failure modes in some sense.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:30.390
You basically follow what
the policy will be doing

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:32.390
and you correct
those failure modes.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.510
Once you've done this, you might
be maybe creating new failure

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.970
modes, or you might be just
observing new failure modes,

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:39.730
and you keep iterating
this process.

00:22:50.950 --> 00:22:53.910
And so, essentially,
DAgger really

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:56.670
operates as a form of
iterative supervised

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:58.870
learning because we're
using supervised learning

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.250
as this inner
learning component.

00:23:01.250 --> 00:23:03.470
But we're really creating
this iterative scheme

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:07.950
where we query the
expert in order

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:11.590
to relabel states that
are visited under evolving

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:13.410
versions of the policy itself.

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:20.160
And if you think about
it at a high level, what

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.360
we're saying here is
that we can see DAgger

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:26.280
as a data efficient
way of querying

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.920
the expert because
we're saying we want

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:34.280
to use the expert as a mechanism
to generate corrective behavior

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:36.760
when the policy fails.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:41.080
And, in this way, we are
directly probing the expert

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:43.040
exactly on those
states that are visited

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:45.300
by the policy where it fails.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:48.520
So here we are basically
following the state distribution

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.600
under the policy,
seeing where it fails

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.880
and relabeling, which is a
pretty data-efficient way

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:59.520
of collecting these
corrective behaviors.

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.600
The other extreme
would be that in order

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:04.760
to collect all the
corrective behaviors that you

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:07.200
care about you would
have to run this maybe

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:11.280
on all the possible states
that your policy would ever

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:12.980
encounter and
relabel everything,

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:16.120
which is extremely expensive.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:18.220
So this is, in a way,
pretty data efficient.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:20.720
But querying the expert
depending on your scenario

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:22.920
is, in general,
something very expensive.

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:26.360
So again, depending on the
task, DAgger might or might not

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:27.540
be something feasible.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:33.000
And so here we looked at
the naive version of DAgger.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:36.120
But there are different
ways that you can maybe

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.240
modify the basic algorithm.

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:42.480
One could be confidence-based
methods whereby here we

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:46.160
are querying the expert on
basically all the states

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.600
that the policy visits
during those rollouts.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:52.160
But we could maybe select
some metric of uncertainty

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:56.440
from the learner itself and only
query the expert on those states

00:24:56.440 --> 00:24:58.920
where the learner was
particularly uncertain

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:00.880
about its answer.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:02.840
The other mechanism is
also, as your colleague

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:05.100
was referring to, is
this human-gated DAgger

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.880
where we have some
humans that are kind

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:10.560
of supervising the
operation of our learner

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:14.010
and directly intervening
when there is a mistake.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.070
Yes.

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:17.290
So if you don't
[INAUDIBLE], is the benefit

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.290
of this trying to
make a lighter weight

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:21.830
policy because you have
an expert policy already,

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:23.450
but you're not using it.

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:25.650
So is this something
that runs faster online?

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:27.410
For example, that
could be one way.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:33.010
So I would say in general, if
you zoom out in all the methods

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:36.370
that we're seeing in this
class, I would say all of these

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:39.010
are irrelevant although
definitely there

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:42.090
is being a shift towards
learning based or more

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:45.170
end to end architectures.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:49.090
The point is that getting
reliable data for these end

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:52.050
to end architecture nowadays,
I would say, even in frontier

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:55.250
models, typically relies on
some more classical approach

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:56.670
to generate labels for those.

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:58.610
And so in some way,
you could see this

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:01.930
as a way of distilling some
maybe extremely expensive

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.930
indirect method
for optimal control

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:08.030
into a fast neural network
that can approximate it.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:20.680
OK, any questions on
DAgger before we move on?

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:32.260
So we saw how DAgger is one
example of an algorithm that

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:36.500
directly tries to target
these covariate shift issues.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:39.660
Let us look at how we could do
this through data collection.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.340
And I want to do this
through two case studies

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:44.860
and going through
two examples of this.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:46.840
So, in this case,
this was, I think,

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:49.920
one of the early works from
NVIDIA on autonomous driving.

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:54.420
I think it dates
back around 2016.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:27:00.980
What they basically tried to
do is learn a neural network

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:07.700
for basically deciding the
steering angle of the car

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:11.030
while receiving as
input some images coming

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:15.610
from some cameras that were
mounted on board the car.

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:22.910
Let's see.

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:26.950
So basically, in this study,
which again was now more or less

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:28.710
10 years ago, what
you would see is

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:30.270
that you started
from something which

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:33.630
was clearly not
working also maybe

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:34.933
being a little bit dangerous.

00:27:34.933 --> 00:27:36.350
But then the point
of this work is

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:39.070
that they started
collecting more data

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.430
for basically more
demonstrations of humans driving

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:46.070
cars and recording
these tuples of images

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.150
and steering angle decisions.

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.910
And they at the end, they
reached a performance

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:53.610
that was to some
extent impressive,

00:27:53.610 --> 00:27:57.190
especially at the time, and
showed some pretty consistent

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:58.910
behavior.

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:02.270
The point is that, of course,
this was pretty exciting

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:03.090
at the time.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:06.630
Nowadays, we have made
clearly some leaps from this.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:09.440
But one thing that I want
to highlight from this work

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:12.140
is something that
you will observe

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.360
if you read through
that paper and look

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:19.000
at the specific framework and
architecture that they designed.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:20.700
Specifically, again,
as we were saying,

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:23.760
the overall problem was
taking some camera images.

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.920
Here they had three,
kind of a center,

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.040
left, and a right camera.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:31.880
They have some recorded steering
wheel angles from human drivers.

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:34.280
And the overall
goal was to learn

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:38.880
convolutional neural network to
basically learn to approximate

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:43.200
and predict those steering
wheel angles from the images.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:46.840
Here I'm glossing over this
box because this is exactly,

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:49.480
I think, interesting
part from data

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:51.880
collection technique
and specifically

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:53.920
data augmentation technique.

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:58.140
So again, pictorially, this is
the kind of situation we are in.

00:28:58.140 --> 00:28:58.740
We have a car.

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:01.520
We have our three
cameras, and we

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:04.840
have one recording of our
steering wheel angle, so the one

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:08.930
that the human
actually executed.

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:12.390
However, if we think about
it a little bit more closely,

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:16.490
the two side cameras could be
used as an example of the car

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:20.170
slightly drifting off
the center of the lane.

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:23.530
And we could use these
observations to fictitiously

00:29:23.530 --> 00:29:27.770
augment the data set with
some steering angles that

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:31.910
are actually seeing
this camera image,

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:33.890
and then correcting
from this camera image

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:37.650
to recenter itself in
the middle of the lane.

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:42.570
So, in general, collecting
data set of driving mistakes

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:44.670
is something that is very
dangerous and expensive.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:48.130
You don't want to be collecting
on purpose corrective data

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.650
where you're
driving off the road

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:51.890
and then going back
again on the road.

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:54.450
And so in this
work, they actually

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:56.370
came up with this
pretty neat trick

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:59.690
whereby they are augmenting
the data they collect

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:04.730
from human drivers to
fictitiously generate

00:30:04.730 --> 00:30:09.970
these corrective data by taking
the side cameras as if they were

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:14.450
front cameras and manually
adjusting the steering

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:16.590
angle for that camera.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:21.770
And so this is, I
think, an example

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:27.690
of how you could try to embed
this corrective behavior not

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:29.310
from a perspective
of the algorithm,

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.870
but from the perspective of data
collection and augmentation.

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:40.250
A very similar idea
was also presented

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:42.810
in this work from the
University of Zurich,

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:47.650
where here the goal was
controlling some quadrotors

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:52.230
to indeed navigate
some hiking trails,

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:57.730
I think, in the Swiss Alps or
mountains from camera images.

00:30:57.730 --> 00:31:01.210
The setting is very
similar to some extent.

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:04.420
We have some image camera,
specifically a front camera

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:06.900
view, and we want
to output this time

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:09.460
a discrete distribution
over actions.

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:13.980
That is, go straight,
turn left, turn right.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:20.660
And once again, very similarly,
what you see in this work

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.140
is something that really
resembles the previous work

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:26.740
from NVIDIA, where here you
had the researchers actually

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:29.360
wearing this fancy
headband with, I think,

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:32.740
three GoPros mounted on
it and collecting data

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:37.260
by literally walking
over hiking trails

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:42.420
and specifically labeling
the actions according

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:44.160
to those same augmentations.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:46.140
So here for the
frontal camera, they

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:48.940
were labeling it as
a go straight action.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:51.900
For the left one, they were
labeling it as a turn right.

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:55.280
And on the right one, they were
labeling as a turn left action.

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:02.230
Indeed, here's the
fancy headline.

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:07.110
And so you would see that this
kind of works in practice.

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:09.110
And so they got something
that was, at the time,

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:12.810
pretty reliable in navigating
those hiking trails.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.050
And so here we saw two examples.

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:24.510
But I think the main message
that I'm trying to bring along

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:28.390
is this idea that we, in trying
to address covariate shift

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:31.830
from a data perspective, we
want to be intentional with data

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:33.170
collection.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.510
And specifically, we
want to intentionally add

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:40.950
mistakes and correction for
those mistakes in our data.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:44.430
So this could be as, in
these examples, where

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:48.690
we want to add some fake
demonstration of corrections

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:51.030
this example of
the side cameras or

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:52.670
from an algorithmic
perspective, we

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:55.350
want to use algorithms
that automate

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:59.400
the collection of this
corrected data as in DAgger.

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:00.680
Yes.

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:04.640
On the prior example,
the drone, made

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:11.560
me wonder when you collecting
this data to train models,

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:16.760
is it possible
[INAUDIBLE] to train

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:19.320
in the case of the
drone moving, train it

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.920
based on the decisions
that are being made?

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:26.200
This is why you go left and
not the tree, while also having

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:29.040
it not be reliant on
the dynamics that are

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.750
inherent to the training data.

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:34.500
Like the way that a human
moves something on their head

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:39.400
[INAUDIBLE], you
can't suddenly fly up

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:42.595
two feet higher
than you are, which

00:33:42.595 --> 00:33:44.720
for a drone that might be
advantageous for the tree

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:47.480
[INAUDIBLE] take
advantage of it.

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:50.240
But if they're trained on the
data that comes from something

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:52.800
that's constrained
to human dynamics,

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:58.330
is there a way to detach the
behavior control of the drone

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:02.570
from the dynamics they are
built into the data to train on?

00:34:02.570 --> 00:34:05.450
I would say not
from the perspective

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:06.670
of imitation learning.

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:11.310
So in imitation learning, we
are, again, at face value,

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:13.610
we are trying to
approximate and imitate

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:16.409
the behavior of some expert.

00:34:16.409 --> 00:34:17.270
And that's about it.

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:20.370
So we don't really
want to go beyond what

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.730
the expert is able to do.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:26.050
And we typically
won't have access

00:34:26.050 --> 00:34:31.590
to data that is beyond what the
expert is able to experience.

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:33.130
So, in general, I would say no.

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:35.570
This will probably
be something that

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:38.530
will be extremely relevant from
the perspective of reinforcement

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:41.090
training, where there
we are not trying

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:44.090
to approximate some
behavior that is being shown

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:45.969
to us by some
experts, but we want

00:34:45.969 --> 00:34:47.510
to interact with
that environment,

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:51.090
maybe a simulator
of the real world

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:53.730
and experiment with
different strategies.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:54.650
Yeah.

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:07.910
And so we covered algorithms.

00:35:07.910 --> 00:35:09.910
We covered data collection.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:14.530
Let us look at using
expressive models to address

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:17.530
or partially solve
the issue that

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:20.673
comes with multimodal behavior.

00:35:20.673 --> 00:35:22.090
And specifically,
we'll be looking

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:24.290
at two main classes of methods.

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:28.690
The first one will be including
history of observations.

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:32.110
We'll see shortly
in the next slide

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:35.730
or using expressive function
classes to better approximate

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:39.650
and capture the
multimodality in our data.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:44.730
So a brief mention on using
history of observations

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:49.050
is that until now, and whenever
we are reframed the MDP

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:52.290
problem theoretically,
we always assume

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:56.940
that the state is a perfect
representation and capturing

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:01.580
all previous history
in our system.

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:05.980
In reality, especially if
we are working with images,

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:09.260
we won't be dealing with
the ground truth full state

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:12.180
representation of our system,
but we will most likely

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:15.620
be dealing with observations
of that system, which are only

00:36:15.620 --> 00:36:18.140
partial informations
or partial observations

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:19.780
of the actual system.

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:25.580
And so, in this case,
there is benefit sometimes

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:30.460
depending on the applications
on rather than defining a policy

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:32.980
as a distribution
over actions given

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:36.900
the instantaneous
observation xt,

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:41.880
in adding a sequence of past
observations to our context,

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.340
essentially.

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:46.160
In the case of
behavioral cloning,

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:49.260
this is particularly
relevant in the case

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:52.870
where we're maybe
collecting data

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:56.590
of these tuples,
states, controls

00:36:56.590 --> 00:36:58.570
from humans' demonstrations.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:00.750
Because if you
think about it, here

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:03.910
if we try to learn
this policy from data,

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:06.590
we are basically assuming
that the human that

00:37:06.590 --> 00:37:11.010
has been collecting data for
us, given the same observation,

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:14.630
will always behave
in the same way.

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:16.670
On the other hand,
history could be

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:18.590
an interesting
confounding factor

00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:21.710
to basically add to our
context to differentiate

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:24.390
between different observations.

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:26.330
Again, let's make
a concrete example.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:30.310
Say that we are x for us is
just the front camera of our car

00:37:30.310 --> 00:37:32.790
and you as our steering angle.

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:36.490
Then if x is only one snapshot
of the current history,

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:40.030
we basically have no idea of
maybe the speed of our car

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:43.430
or any other relevant
measure that could

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.910
be useful for decision-making.

00:37:45.910 --> 00:37:51.440
And we are implicitly assuming
that given the same front car

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:54.600
camera image, we would be
always behaving in the same way.

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:58.160
On the other hand,
if we assume to have

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.620
a history of previous
camera images,

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:02.960
then we might be capturing
more useful information

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:05.600
for decision-making.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:10.040
In practice, this is typically
handled through basically

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:13.260
your favorite sequence model
where it could be transformers,

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:16.600
could be recurrent neural
nets, or something of the sort

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:19.600
where the policy then
basically becomes

00:38:19.600 --> 00:38:23.040
something that takes a sequence
of states or observations

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:25.640
and outputs the next action.

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:29.380
So again, pretty high
level discussion,

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:31.400
but I think it's
worth mentioning.

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:33.840
In practice, I would
say also my experience

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:39.480
with including history in
defining the behavior policies

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:41.060
is usually very empirical.

00:38:41.060 --> 00:38:43.560
So you will be doing
some sort of ablation

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:47.770
where you're maybe trying
out the training, your policy

00:38:47.770 --> 00:38:54.390
on your same data set with
varying number of history time

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:56.870
steps in the past and
seeing what works best.

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:07.610
OK, but let us move
to, I think, something

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:10.490
that is more
fundamental, which is

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:15.370
this issue of wanting to capture
the full data distribution.

00:39:15.370 --> 00:39:17.970
So we made before this
example of the drone flying

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:18.850
past the tree.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:21.850
And we could have these two
equivalent solution modes

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:25.730
or behavior modes
for our policy.

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:28.890
And so the goal for
us rather than fitting

00:39:28.890 --> 00:39:33.090
this mean value of this
potentially multi-modal

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:35.490
distribution, what we
want to do is represent

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:38.610
the entire distribution.

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:41.890
And to do so, let us briefly
revisit what it actually

00:39:41.890 --> 00:39:47.610
means for us to parameterize
or devise a parametric function

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.690
to express full distributions.

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:53.090
And this will loosely depend on
whether the distribution that we

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:56.890
want to output is
discrete, so something

00:39:56.890 --> 00:40:00.330
like categorical distribution
over a finite set

00:40:00.330 --> 00:40:04.050
of possible actions, say the
four cardinal directions moving

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:05.850
up, down, left, or right.

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:08.170
Or if this is
continuous, it could

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:12.450
be something like capturing the
mean and the standard deviation

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:15.570
of what our desired
steering angle is.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.730
In practice, how
we do this is we

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:20.610
would say that we wish to
define a neural network that

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:23.310
outputs these parameters
of our distributions.

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:27.030
We could either have a
neural net output the logits

00:40:27.030 --> 00:40:29.530
or the individual
probabilities for each

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:31.210
of our discrete actions.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:34.010
Or we could have a neural
net that basically represents

00:40:34.010 --> 00:40:36.170
the mean and standard
deviation of a candidate

00:40:36.170 --> 00:40:39.350
Gaussian over possible
steering angle commands.

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:46.660
The benefit of the
discrete distribution

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:50.780
is that it naturally
handles multi-modality.

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:56.060
So say that, again, this
is the distribution that we

00:40:56.060 --> 00:41:00.400
wish to have our model output.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:06.580
If we discretize this naturally
continuous state into bins

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:09.760
and then assign a probability
to each of those bins,

00:41:09.760 --> 00:41:11.100
that sums up to 1.

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:15.980
We might be very well able to
capture this multi-modality

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:18.920
by learning something
of the sort.

00:41:21.860 --> 00:41:24.780
So this discretized
representations

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:27.660
are actually very
powerful because it's

00:41:27.660 --> 00:41:29.320
something we can do very well.

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:31.780
So we could just learn this
through cross entropy, which

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:33.940
is pretty standard in
supervised learning

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:36.860
and get out of this something
that naturally represents

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:40.060
multimodal distributions.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:44.030
At the same time, I think
pretty obvious downside

00:41:44.030 --> 00:41:47.590
is that depending on the
dimensionality of our output

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:49.850
and the discretization
that we are interested in,

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:52.550
and that is maybe relevant
for our application,

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:55.710
we could have a lot
of bins to-- learn

00:41:55.710 --> 00:42:00.290
and a lot of probability
kind of logits to estimate.

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:03.550
And so this scales poorly with
the dimensionality of our action

00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:04.930
space, in this case.

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:11.910
The pro of using a continuous
distribution as our output

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:14.350
representation is obviously
that this naturally

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:17.490
captures the continuous
nature of our actions,

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.470
so no discretization required.

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:22.710
But this might not
be very expressive.

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:25.390
So if we wish to use
a Gaussian and we

00:42:25.390 --> 00:42:31.310
want to fit a Gaussian to
this real underlying bimodal

00:42:31.310 --> 00:42:34.430
distribution, then
probably what we will learn

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:39.310
is something like this.

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:40.840
And this is because
we are trying

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:42.880
to represent a
bimodal distribution

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:45.060
through a unimodal distribution.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.140
Does this make sense?

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:55.760
Any questions or doubts here?

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:56.580
Yes.

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:01.960
Is the right scaling [INAUDIBLE]
distribution as well?

00:43:01.960 --> 00:43:03.000
Sorry, can you repeat?

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:05.440
For higher dimensional
[INAUDIBLE] distributions,

00:43:05.440 --> 00:43:09.600
does it also not
scale [INAUDIBLE]?

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:11.140
The continuous distribution?

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:14.480
Of course, depending
on the distribution

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:18.080
that you care about, this
might or might not scale well.

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:21.120
In general, like say we are
just looking at an n dimensional

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:22.000
Gaussian.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:23.840
This is pretty
compactly represented

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:27.560
by a mean vector and a
covariance matrix, which

00:43:27.560 --> 00:43:30.760
might be much cheaper
compared to having

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:34.100
a binning in n
dimensional space.

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:39.290
Could you also account
for different modes

00:43:39.290 --> 00:43:41.330
by just adding one [INAUDIBLE]?

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:44.030
Yes, exactly.

00:43:44.030 --> 00:43:48.342
So this is a great
segue to the next slide.

00:43:48.342 --> 00:43:51.744
I'm not even [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:58.530
And so, indeed, the point
is so these are ways

00:43:58.530 --> 00:44:01.850
of representing distributions.

00:44:01.850 --> 00:44:04.090
How can we be more
expressive in representing

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:06.050
multimodal distributions?

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:09.610
One example could be, as
you were exactly saying,

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:13.010
let's fit a Gaussian
mixture model to our data.

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:16.570
What is a Gaussian Mixture
Model or GMM for short?

00:44:16.570 --> 00:44:18.730
Is that we're basically
defining rather

00:44:18.730 --> 00:44:20.610
than a single
unimodal Gaussian, we

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:23.010
want to learn the
parameters of a mixture

00:44:23.010 --> 00:44:25.690
of Gaussian distributions,
which is usually

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:28.250
comprised by k Gaussians.

00:44:28.250 --> 00:44:34.690
So we will be defining the mean
and covariance matrix for each

00:44:34.690 --> 00:44:39.020
of those k Gaussians together
with a weighting factor

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:43.900
or at least k weighting factors
w1 to wk, which basically

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:49.620
define the weighted average
of these individual Gaussians.

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:51.940
So, in practice, the Gaussian
mixture distributions

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:55.420
are quite powerful
and expressive

00:44:55.420 --> 00:45:00.340
because through Gaussians,
we can easily approximate

00:45:00.340 --> 00:45:02.640
potentially complex functions.

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:09.500
So we could have, indeed,
these three Gaussians

00:45:09.500 --> 00:45:13.060
being somehow combined
through appropriate choice

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:17.180
of the weights to maybe measure
to represent a distribution that

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:19.180
is extremely non-unimodal.

00:45:24.184 --> 00:45:26.660
A caveat, of course, in
Gaussian mixture models

00:45:26.660 --> 00:45:29.220
is that the choice of
k is a hyperparameter

00:45:29.220 --> 00:45:32.620
and has to be chosen in advance.

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:35.200
Again, this is usually
something pretty empirical.

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:37.100
That is just try
different numbers of k

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:39.380
and see what works best.

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:42.180
The trade-offs here
could be the usual ones

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:45.340
that come with respect to
overfitting and underfitting

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:46.220
of data.

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:49.340
If I have very high k, I might
be fitting very well my training

00:45:49.340 --> 00:45:53.180
data, but this doesn't really
generalize to my testing data

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:55.580
or inference.

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:58.940
Or we could define k based
on some domain knowledge.

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:00.860
So we may know that
the policy actually

00:46:00.860 --> 00:46:02.680
has three distinct modes.

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:04.840
And so we might
just pick that as k.

00:46:04.840 --> 00:46:06.880
But, in general, it's
a hyperparameter.

00:46:12.260 --> 00:46:18.140
A second class of approaches
to represent or expressive

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:20.900
ways of representing
multi-modal distributions

00:46:20.900 --> 00:46:23.700
is what I'm referring to
here as the discretized plus

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:25.780
autoregressive approach.

00:46:25.780 --> 00:46:30.380
So we introduced before
this idea of discretization

00:46:30.380 --> 00:46:32.760
to represent multi-modal
distributions.

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:36.270
But we mentioned that scaling
this to high dimensional actions

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:39.030
for us, or in general, high
dimensional distributions,

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:41.630
could be expensive.

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:45.430
Again, I think a nice
visualization could be something

00:46:45.430 --> 00:46:52.830
where say that we have our
two dimensional distribution

00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:54.550
that we wish to estimate.

00:46:54.550 --> 00:46:57.590
The discretization in this
case would essentially

00:46:57.590 --> 00:47:01.430
be equivalent to picking a
discretization in our two

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:05.750
dimensional space and having to
pick a specific value for each

00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:08.150
of these grid cells.

00:47:08.150 --> 00:47:10.933
You see how this
scales very badly.

00:47:10.933 --> 00:47:12.350
And specifically,
not only does it

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:15.510
scale badly in the sense
of number of parameters

00:47:15.510 --> 00:47:19.310
that we want to
estimate, it also

00:47:19.310 --> 00:47:21.830
clearly suffers from the
curse of dimensionality

00:47:21.830 --> 00:47:24.550
when we look at model learning.

00:47:24.550 --> 00:47:26.790
And this is because
in reality, we only

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:31.120
be estimating our
distribution from samples

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:33.540
from our underlying
distribution.

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:37.640
And so in order to populate
all of the grid cells,

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:40.520
you see how we would need an
exponentially large number

00:47:40.520 --> 00:47:43.480
of samples from
our distribution.

00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:47.440
So a neat trick is one
of basically decomposing

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:52.020
autoregressively this
two-dimensional distribution.

00:47:56.080 --> 00:47:57.080
What does this mean?

00:47:57.080 --> 00:48:03.120
In general, we could represent
exactly a joint distribution

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:15.990
over x1 and x2 as
basically the product

00:48:15.990 --> 00:48:20.580
of the conditional
distribution, x1 times--

00:48:20.580 --> 00:48:24.780
so the probability of x1 times
the probability of x2 given x1.

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:30.930
And this is an
exact reformulation.

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:33.010
So we're not losing anything.

00:48:33.010 --> 00:48:35.690
But what we're
gaining is that rather

00:48:35.690 --> 00:48:40.770
than having to discretize
n dimensional space,

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:43.810
we only do one dimensional
discretization.

00:48:43.810 --> 00:48:45.970
So, in practice, this
would mean something

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:53.490
like we only need to discretize
the one dimensional vector.

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:55.770
So this would be like p of x1.

00:48:55.770 --> 00:49:00.970
And this would be
p of x2 given x1.

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:04.730
And only have to learn these
two one-dimensional discretized

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:05.830
representations.

00:49:10.610 --> 00:49:13.170
And here not only this is more
efficient from the perspective

00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:16.570
of total parameter count, it is
also more efficient with respect

00:49:16.570 --> 00:49:18.650
to the course of
dimensionality issue

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:20.850
that we were describing
because here.

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:24.970
While this the samples in this
2D grid are pretty sparse,

00:49:24.970 --> 00:49:29.660
the same four samples would
be clearly denser in these one

00:49:29.660 --> 00:49:35.580
dimensional vectors because we
would be using the same four

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:37.240
samples, something like that.

00:49:46.055 --> 00:49:48.540
In practice, this is
exactly what people

00:49:48.540 --> 00:49:52.900
do when using transformer
models for robot policies.

00:49:52.900 --> 00:49:58.820
So in this case, you would
be your one step probability

00:49:58.820 --> 00:50:01.420
distribution would just
be the transformer.

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:04.220
You would have some context
which could be maybe some camera

00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:06.500
image, maybe some
natural language

00:50:06.500 --> 00:50:08.460
prompts that describe your task.

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:10.540
And your learning,
your distribution

00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:14.620
over next element in
that sequence where

00:50:14.620 --> 00:50:18.340
this could
essentially be, again,

00:50:18.340 --> 00:50:20.820
let us look at
this more visually.

00:50:20.820 --> 00:50:25.180
So, in natural language,
in LLMs, you typically

00:50:25.180 --> 00:50:36.940
have some transformer block
that takes an input like a token

00:50:36.940 --> 00:50:41.100
here, maybe a sequence of
those, and basically predicts

00:50:41.100 --> 00:50:44.100
the next token in that sequence.

00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:47.620
We can just call it maybe xt.

00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:49.140
In order to predict
this, then you

00:50:49.140 --> 00:50:53.660
would be autoregressively
introducing the predicted xt

00:50:53.660 --> 00:50:58.080
here, predict xt
plus 1, and so forth,

00:50:58.080 --> 00:51:01.740
kind of doing this
autoregressive prediction.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:05.260
Here we would be taking
this exact framework.

00:51:05.260 --> 00:51:08.860
That is, we only learn this
one step prediction rule

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:13.500
where xt, rather than being
tokens or words as you would

00:51:13.500 --> 00:51:18.220
do in LLMs, here, the xt could
be one dimension of our action.

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:23.300
So if we have an
action vector u that

00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:31.310
is comprised of like a steering
angle and an acceleration,

00:51:31.310 --> 00:51:35.510
we might have this
decomposed such that we first

00:51:35.510 --> 00:51:37.150
predict the steering
angle and then

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:39.590
we feed the steering
angle back to our model

00:51:39.590 --> 00:51:41.790
and predict the acceleration.

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:43.990
Does that make sense?

00:51:43.990 --> 00:51:45.270
Yes.

00:51:45.270 --> 00:51:50.158
[INAUDIBLE] you have a lot of
stuff like that dimensionality.

00:51:50.158 --> 00:51:51.950
You still have to go
through and understand

00:51:51.950 --> 00:51:54.990
why everything [INAUDIBLE]
to everything else before you

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:59.248
can run this [INAUDIBLE]
and narrow things down?

00:51:59.248 --> 00:52:01.290
Yeah, I would say in terms
of dimensionality, no.

00:52:01.290 --> 00:52:04.510
But here basically
trading off ease

00:52:04.510 --> 00:52:07.710
of representation with compute
because now you would not

00:52:07.710 --> 00:52:09.710
be sampling all at
once, but you're

00:52:09.710 --> 00:52:11.530
sampling autoregressively.

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:14.110
So in order to generate
the full vector, which

00:52:14.110 --> 00:52:17.990
might be n dimensional, you
would have to do n steps,

00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:21.010
n calls to, in this
case, transformer block.

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:34.960
Again, so this is a very,
very popular practice.

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:39.420
Another class of models,
also very popular,

00:52:39.420 --> 00:52:41.800
especially over the
last couple of years,

00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:44.480
is basically relates
with diffusion processes

00:52:44.480 --> 00:52:45.800
and diffusion models.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:48.280
And even more recently, I
would say over the last couple

00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:50.960
of years, flow matching.

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:56.880
And the idea of diffusion
in general is that--

00:52:56.880 --> 00:53:00.840
so we're saying we want to
sample from a potentially

00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:06.640
complex distribution
but obviously, we

00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:10.320
don't know how to sample
from this potentially wild

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:12.120
distribution.

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:14.860
But, in general, we might
have some easy distribution,

00:53:14.860 --> 00:53:23.110
say a Gaussian from which we
know exactly how to sample.

00:53:23.110 --> 00:53:24.210
And this is easy.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:28.690
And so in the diffusion
processes in general cast

00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:32.770
the problem as wanting
to transform a sample

00:53:32.770 --> 00:53:34.530
from a simple
distribution, which

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:37.130
we know how to sample
from, and then transform

00:53:37.130 --> 00:53:40.490
these samples such that they
match the target distribution

00:53:40.490 --> 00:53:42.730
that we care about learning.

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:48.090
And so, in practice,
what diffusion typically

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:52.950
defines is a process
whereby we at sampling time

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:56.890
we would go from noise
to the actual realization

00:53:56.890 --> 00:53:58.230
that we care about.

00:53:58.230 --> 00:54:02.770
So we want to learn
this transformation.

00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:07.550
Diffusion models were originally
introduced for generating images

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:10.130
so we would say
nowadays, basically,

00:54:10.130 --> 00:54:11.930
any image generation
out there is

00:54:11.930 --> 00:54:14.890
using some form of diffusion
model or a flow matching

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:16.850
representation.

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:20.900
And the goal here is
to map from pure noise

00:54:20.900 --> 00:54:23.780
given some conditioning,
for example, generate

00:54:23.780 --> 00:54:27.980
an image of a cat
to the actual image

00:54:27.980 --> 00:54:29.620
that we care about generating.

00:54:29.620 --> 00:54:32.800
So the sampling direction
is pretty straightforward.

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:33.920
We want to take noise.

00:54:33.920 --> 00:54:39.160
We have a learned kind
of transformation.

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:43.180
And we use this to gradually
and iteratively denoise going

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:45.020
from noise to actual image.

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:47.240
The tricky one and
interesting is, of course,

00:54:47.240 --> 00:54:49.220
the backward direction.

00:54:49.220 --> 00:54:52.260
So how do we learn this mapping?

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:54.720
In practice, for
diffusion models,

00:54:54.720 --> 00:54:59.380
this is done by learning this
individual denoising step.

00:54:59.380 --> 00:55:00.900
So what you would
do is you would

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:05.940
take your data set of images
and define gradually more noisy

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:07.780
versions of those images.

00:55:07.780 --> 00:55:14.700
So we would take x0 and then
define x0 plus some noise

00:55:14.700 --> 00:55:18.310
and then define--
and this would be x1.

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:20.870
And then we define
x1 plus some noise.

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:22.270
This would be x2.

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:25.030
And so we gradually
generate more and more noisy

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:29.150
versions of this image
until we get pure noise.

00:55:29.150 --> 00:55:31.070
And the goal in
diffusion models we then

00:55:31.070 --> 00:55:35.110
want to approximate
this noise term to

00:55:35.110 --> 00:55:38.670
basically being able
to reverse the process.

00:55:38.670 --> 00:55:42.555
So at data generation time we
basically have the forward,

00:55:42.555 --> 00:55:44.430
generate the forward
generation where we just

00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:45.990
add noise to our image.

00:55:45.990 --> 00:55:49.350
And then we want to
approximate this noise

00:55:49.350 --> 00:55:52.890
through a learned parametric
mapping, f of theta,

00:55:52.890 --> 00:55:57.190
which then allows us to go
in the reverse direction.

00:55:57.190 --> 00:56:00.070
Again, I'm aware this is
pretty hand-wavy description,

00:56:00.070 --> 00:56:01.830
but I'm happy to
provide references

00:56:01.830 --> 00:56:03.730
for diffusion
models in practice.

00:56:03.730 --> 00:56:06.590
But the bottom line here is
that diffusion models and flow

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:09.070
matching models are
extremely powerful ways

00:56:09.070 --> 00:56:13.990
of representing these
multi-modal distributions.

00:56:13.990 --> 00:56:18.430
But let us look a little bit
more closely into flow matching.

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:21.390
And the idea is that
if in diffusion models

00:56:21.390 --> 00:56:25.730
we are interested in learning
this error term epsilon,

00:56:25.730 --> 00:56:32.350
this noise term epsilon to be
able to reverse the denoising

00:56:32.350 --> 00:56:35.870
process, in flow matching, what
we want to learn essentially

00:56:35.870 --> 00:56:39.090
is a vector field to do
this iterative denoising.

00:56:39.090 --> 00:56:43.030
So we're basically describing
this statistical transport

00:56:43.030 --> 00:56:47.310
problem where we want to move
samples from a simple noise

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:50.150
distribution into samples
from a potentially

00:56:50.150 --> 00:56:53.190
complex distribution.

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:55.710
So, in practice, at
inference the way this goes

00:56:55.710 --> 00:57:00.990
is that assume we have learned
a vector field v of theta.

00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:04.870
At inference, we would
be sampling point x0

00:57:04.870 --> 00:57:06.870
from our noise distribution.

00:57:06.870 --> 00:57:11.430
And then we would generating
a data point from our target

00:57:11.430 --> 00:57:15.240
distribution essentially entails
integrating forward this vector

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:17.400
field.

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:22.280
And we can do this through
Euler integration, for example.

00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:25.360
Again, the caveat here
is how do we train

00:57:25.360 --> 00:57:27.680
and how do we learn
this vector field?

00:57:27.680 --> 00:57:30.240
And at training time,
what we would be doing

00:57:30.240 --> 00:57:32.540
is that we sample a couple.

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:37.440
So we would sample a
sample x0 from a candidate

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:39.680
initial distribution,
say a Gaussian.

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:43.260
We would sample a point, x1,
from our data set, that is,

00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:45.040
a sample from the
target distribution

00:57:45.040 --> 00:57:46.920
that we care about learning.

00:57:46.920 --> 00:57:50.280
We would sample a
time step, t, usually

00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:53.700
from a uniform distribution
between 0 and 1

00:57:53.700 --> 00:57:58.720
because we are assuming that our
denoising step goes from time 0

00:57:58.720 --> 00:58:01.080
to time 1.

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:04.440
And then in flow
matching, we basically

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:07.240
linearly interpolate
between these two points.

00:58:07.240 --> 00:58:10.320
This would essentially mean
drawing a straight line

00:58:10.320 --> 00:58:13.970
from our sample to the
target sample from the target

00:58:13.970 --> 00:58:15.250
distribution.

00:58:15.250 --> 00:58:20.970
And we basically update the
parameters of our learned vector

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:26.570
field, such as to minimize the
distance between our prediction

00:58:26.570 --> 00:58:28.930
of the velocity,
essentially, with the target

00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:30.930
velocity between
those two points

00:58:30.930 --> 00:58:35.090
because x1 minus x0 is
essentially the target velocity.

00:58:39.010 --> 00:58:49.850
And so, in practice,
what this would

00:58:49.850 --> 00:58:53.950
look like is something where
we have our easy distribution,

00:58:53.950 --> 00:58:56.170
say a normal distribution here.

00:58:56.170 --> 00:58:59.850
We have our complex
distribution here.

00:58:59.850 --> 00:59:02.490
And what we would
do is we essentially

00:59:02.490 --> 00:59:09.340
learn a vector field that kind
of guides us from every point

00:59:09.340 --> 00:59:13.040
into sampling from
this distribution.

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:15.900
Of course, imagine this
vector field to be complete.

00:59:15.900 --> 00:59:17.620
But then, in
practice, we would be

00:59:17.620 --> 00:59:21.540
sampling from our distribution
and just integrating forward,

00:59:21.540 --> 00:59:24.820
for example, through
Euler integration

00:59:24.820 --> 00:59:28.720
our samples to generate samples
from this other distribution.

00:59:34.220 --> 00:59:37.300
And I would say flow matching
is very popular these days

00:59:37.300 --> 00:59:40.460
because it's extremely
powerful at representing

00:59:40.460 --> 00:59:43.020
continuous distributions,
which are ultimately

00:59:43.020 --> 00:59:47.040
what we care about in
robot control, for example.

00:59:53.340 --> 00:59:54.400
Any questions here?

00:59:59.380 --> 01:00:05.580
OK, our last trick that I
wanted to make sure we covered

01:00:05.580 --> 01:00:07.770
is the one of action chunking.

01:00:07.770 --> 01:00:12.530
So up until now and
throughout this course,

01:00:12.530 --> 01:00:16.590
we've always been
discussing feedback policy

01:00:16.590 --> 01:00:24.190
in the form of a distribution
over next controls given states.

01:00:24.190 --> 01:00:26.270
And so, in general,
the framework

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:28.990
is something where we
observe some state xt.

01:00:28.990 --> 01:00:31.190
We had some policy
from which allows

01:00:31.190 --> 01:00:35.790
us to basically sample an
action in that specific state.

01:00:35.790 --> 01:00:38.410
We would be executing this
action in the environment,

01:00:38.410 --> 01:00:42.390
and then we would observe a new
state and repeat this process.

01:00:42.390 --> 01:00:43.950
And so, visually,
this would look

01:00:43.950 --> 01:00:47.930
like at all time
steps from 0 to 7,

01:00:47.930 --> 01:00:50.630
we would be
generating one action,

01:00:50.630 --> 01:00:53.230
executing that in that
respective time steps

01:00:53.230 --> 01:00:56.310
and moving forward over time.

01:00:56.310 --> 01:00:58.950
Action chunking, over
which I would say right now

01:00:58.950 --> 01:01:02.750
is the most popular
way of parameterizing,

01:01:02.750 --> 01:01:05.230
kind of robot learning
policies is something

01:01:05.230 --> 01:01:08.170
where rather than
predicting a single action.

01:01:08.170 --> 01:01:11.790
We are basically predicting
actions over a specific planning

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.950
horizon of length, k.

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:16.470
So, if you wish, this
looks like something

01:01:16.470 --> 01:01:18.470
like a model predictive control.

01:01:18.470 --> 01:01:21.790
So we're basically predicting
k actions in the future

01:01:21.790 --> 01:01:27.210
and executing those k
actions in our environment.

01:01:30.390 --> 01:01:32.910
So this became pretty
popular, first of all,

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:34.610
because it tends
to work quite well.

01:01:34.610 --> 01:01:36.190
So people just adopted it.

01:01:36.190 --> 01:01:39.750
In general, I would say that
a clear benefit is that this

01:01:39.750 --> 01:01:42.070
allows more time for inference.

01:01:42.070 --> 01:01:45.830
So if we have pre-computed the
actions for the next k time

01:01:45.830 --> 01:01:49.670
steps, then we can just trust
our predictions for the next k

01:01:49.670 --> 01:01:53.330
steps and only have to
recompute new ones after k.

01:01:56.470 --> 01:01:59.910
Another more subtle
motivation for which

01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:02.990
it is believed that these models
perform, and it has been shown

01:02:02.990 --> 01:02:04.600
that these models
perform better,

01:02:04.600 --> 01:02:07.360
is that in general,
this leads to smoother

01:02:07.360 --> 01:02:09.600
controls trajectories.

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:13.300
So if you look again at
what we're doing here,

01:02:13.300 --> 01:02:17.560
typically our policy, pi, would
be a learned object pi of theta.

01:02:17.560 --> 01:02:20.200
And we would be calling
this object, essentially,

01:02:20.200 --> 01:02:21.840
at each time step.

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:25.840
And since this learned
policy might not be perfect,

01:02:25.840 --> 01:02:28.580
the learning signal might
be a little bit jittery

01:02:28.580 --> 01:02:30.040
because we might be--

01:02:30.040 --> 01:02:34.480
we are basically introducing
some randomness at every call.

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:38.960
If we, however, predict a
full prediction sequence,

01:02:38.960 --> 01:02:42.900
then within this short
horizon sequence,

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:44.320
typically the
generations are more

01:02:44.320 --> 01:02:47.480
coherent between each other.

01:02:47.480 --> 01:02:49.720
And so this leads
to smoother controls

01:02:49.720 --> 01:02:56.080
which typically are deployed
with better performance.

01:02:56.080 --> 01:03:00.520
But this is definitely another
trick of the trade, if you wish,

01:03:00.520 --> 01:03:04.730
that is worth knowing about.

01:03:04.730 --> 01:03:06.970
And so some examples.

01:03:06.970 --> 01:03:10.970
This work, diffusion policy
was pretty popular probably

01:03:10.970 --> 01:03:13.850
now dating back three
or four years ago.

01:03:13.850 --> 01:03:18.370
It was one of the first examples
of leveraging diffusion models

01:03:18.370 --> 01:03:21.130
and diffusion policies at scale.

01:03:21.130 --> 01:03:26.130
So, in general, here we're going
from an image representation

01:03:26.130 --> 01:03:29.450
observation of our state
and using diffusion models

01:03:29.450 --> 01:03:36.970
to generate a full trajectory
of behavior for our robotic arm.

01:03:36.970 --> 01:03:40.650
And it was shown to be able to
do extremely challenging tasks

01:03:40.650 --> 01:03:42.990
generalize across
different conditions.

01:03:42.990 --> 01:03:47.250
And so the results here
were very good and exciting

01:03:47.250 --> 01:03:48.920
at the time.

01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:50.670
So not only are we
using diffusion models,

01:03:50.670 --> 01:03:53.350
you also notice that here we're
using this action chunking idea.

01:03:53.350 --> 01:03:55.650
So we're generating a full
trajectory rather than

01:03:55.650 --> 01:03:58.370
a single action.

01:03:58.370 --> 01:04:05.940
And so this is definitely a work
that I suggest you go check out.

01:04:05.940 --> 01:04:09.100
Another example would be the
kind of robotics transformer

01:04:09.100 --> 01:04:11.060
series of works.

01:04:11.060 --> 01:04:13.420
There have been follow
ups to this work.

01:04:13.420 --> 01:04:15.260
But at the high
level here, I wanted

01:04:15.260 --> 01:04:18.660
to show the case
where in general, you

01:04:18.660 --> 01:04:22.140
are learning a mapping in the
form of a transformer model

01:04:22.140 --> 01:04:26.020
that maps from a
history of images.

01:04:26.020 --> 01:04:30.000
So again, if you think back
about our multiple time step,

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:33.860
multiple observations of
history and a language

01:04:33.860 --> 01:04:35.540
description of the
task that we wish

01:04:35.540 --> 01:04:41.700
to execute to discretized
representation of our action

01:04:41.700 --> 01:04:43.980
where we have been
essentially doing

01:04:43.980 --> 01:04:47.720
this discretization plus
autoregressive generation.

01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:54.600
So here the arm had different
actions that it had to execute.

01:04:54.600 --> 01:04:57.580
And we are basically in
this work they basically

01:04:57.580 --> 01:05:00.430
generated each of these
dimensions individually

01:05:00.430 --> 01:05:04.070
and conditioning on the
previous one to do so.

01:05:04.070 --> 01:05:06.432
So these are just
examples of the topics

01:05:06.432 --> 01:05:07.890
that we have been
discussing today.

01:05:10.790 --> 01:05:14.430
And so at a high
level, I would say

01:05:14.430 --> 01:05:17.870
that we have been touching
these common pitfalls

01:05:17.870 --> 01:05:21.470
and possible strategies to
remedy for these pitfalls that

01:05:21.470 --> 01:05:25.450
range from algorithms that
are targeting those pitfalls,

01:05:25.450 --> 01:05:28.350
for example, DAgger, we looked
at SMART data collection

01:05:28.350 --> 01:05:30.990
and augmentations,
take the three

01:05:30.990 --> 01:05:34.350
cameras and the
fictitious augmentations.

01:05:34.350 --> 01:05:38.110
And we had a big
parenthesis on, how can we

01:05:38.110 --> 01:05:40.650
represent the full
distribution of our data?

01:05:40.650 --> 01:05:42.230
Because this is
ultimately what we

01:05:42.230 --> 01:05:44.610
care about when learning
from demonstrations.

01:05:48.310 --> 01:05:50.510
And so I would say
that we have seen

01:05:50.510 --> 01:05:56.560
behavior cloning and a usual,
a classical benefit and pro

01:05:56.560 --> 01:06:00.300
of behavior cloning
is that at its core,

01:06:00.300 --> 01:06:03.120
it's essentially supervised
learning plus or minus some kind

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:04.000
of deltas.

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:06.400
And I would say supervised
learning is definitely

01:06:06.400 --> 01:06:07.980
easy to implement and monitor.

01:06:07.980 --> 01:06:08.980
We have good metrics.

01:06:08.980 --> 01:06:11.560
We know it's usually well
behaved from the perspective

01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:13.000
of gradient descent.

01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:17.120
And so this is usually
pretty simple to implement.

01:06:17.120 --> 01:06:18.580
It's also pretty efficient.

01:06:18.580 --> 01:06:21.300
So there is no need for
trial and error learning,

01:06:21.300 --> 01:06:22.720
as we will see
starting Wednesday

01:06:22.720 --> 01:06:24.960
in reinforcement learning.

01:06:24.960 --> 01:06:27.540
And there is no need for
an explicit reward signal.

01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:30.360
So in some cases,
defining a reward

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:35.840
might be harder than learning
to devising a policy itself.

01:06:35.840 --> 01:06:37.680
Take the case of driving.

01:06:37.680 --> 01:06:40.680
How do we quantify numerically
what good driving is?

01:06:40.680 --> 01:06:42.880
On the other hand,
learning a policy

01:06:42.880 --> 01:06:44.800
to mimic expert
demonstrations of driving

01:06:44.800 --> 01:06:48.840
is actually much more tractable.

01:06:48.840 --> 01:06:53.080
On the cons side, of
course, all of this

01:06:53.080 --> 01:06:56.840
assumes that we have a strong
dependency on the quality

01:06:56.840 --> 01:06:58.020
of the demonstrations.

01:06:58.020 --> 01:07:00.000
So if the
demonstrations are bad,

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:05.080
then there is no way that the
learned policy will be good.

01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:08.200
Of course, these strategies
that we discussed today

01:07:08.200 --> 01:07:12.220
are ways of addressing the
compounding errors problems.

01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:14.560
But these problems remain.

01:07:14.560 --> 01:07:17.600
And, in general, generalization
of learned policies

01:07:17.600 --> 01:07:20.000
is a big topic.

01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:24.500
And linked with the first
one, in behavior cloning,

01:07:24.500 --> 01:07:26.760
there is no
exploration whatsoever.

01:07:26.760 --> 01:07:31.080
So we will have no way of
discovering new solutions

01:07:31.080 --> 01:07:37.440
for the task that have not been
demonstrated from an expert.

01:07:37.440 --> 01:07:39.520
And so this is where
reinforcement learning

01:07:39.520 --> 01:07:43.280
will come in the picture.

01:07:43.280 --> 01:07:45.980
Any questions on
behavior cloning?

01:07:51.400 --> 01:07:55.850
So I want to quickly touch
on a couple of actually one

01:07:55.850 --> 01:07:57.610
other paradigm for
behavioral cloning

01:07:57.610 --> 01:08:00.250
and then give some pointers
to inverse reinforcement

01:08:00.250 --> 01:08:03.290
learning methods.

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:09.810
So one interesting
idea is that, can we

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:14.810
use ideas from
behavior cloning to try

01:08:14.810 --> 01:08:18.370
to approximate the objective
of reinforcement learning?

01:08:18.370 --> 01:08:21.850
That is, can we go
beyond or at least

01:08:21.850 --> 01:08:26.210
filter out the performance
of demonstrations

01:08:26.210 --> 01:08:30.529
in our data set such that we
are able to get as close as

01:08:30.529 --> 01:08:33.410
possible to the best
kind of performance that

01:08:33.410 --> 01:08:38.010
has been shown in the data set
and potentially improve on it?

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:41.410
And so the idea
here is that rather

01:08:41.410 --> 01:08:49.090
than relying on solely
optimal demonstrations

01:08:49.090 --> 01:08:52.140
for a specific task, we may
want to leverage a broader

01:08:52.140 --> 01:08:55.080
set of demonstrations,
for example,

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:58.819
from suboptimal policies
or related tasks

01:08:58.819 --> 01:09:04.060
or things of the sort and
slightly shift the learning

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:07.020
problem that we are
positing in order

01:09:07.020 --> 01:09:09.819
to get the best from our data.

01:09:09.819 --> 01:09:11.740
And I would say two
main classes of methods

01:09:11.740 --> 01:09:14.700
are the filtering or
weighting of demonstrations

01:09:14.700 --> 01:09:19.075
or devising different
conditionings for our policy.

01:09:19.075 --> 01:09:20.700
So let us go into
the details because I

01:09:20.700 --> 01:09:24.340
think this will be clearer.

01:09:24.340 --> 01:09:26.220
So, in general, we
have been discussing

01:09:26.220 --> 01:09:28.340
the idea of behavioral
cloning completely detached

01:09:28.340 --> 01:09:29.439
from performance.

01:09:29.439 --> 01:09:32.300
We had no measure of
reward that was scoring

01:09:32.300 --> 01:09:34.859
how good the trajectory was
with respect to another.

01:09:34.859 --> 01:09:37.960
And so a kind of
very simple idea is,

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:41.740
can we introduce some
of these considerations

01:09:41.740 --> 01:09:47.819
by essentially reintroducing
the reward function

01:09:47.819 --> 01:09:50.310
as a player in this game?

01:09:50.310 --> 01:09:54.830
Whereby we might, for
example, a simple idea

01:09:54.830 --> 01:09:56.450
would just be doing filtering.

01:09:56.450 --> 01:09:59.270
So say we have a reward
function that is able to score

01:09:59.270 --> 01:10:02.390
a specific action in a
how good was an action

01:10:02.390 --> 01:10:04.870
ut in a specific state.

01:10:04.870 --> 01:10:08.470
This may allow us to score the
quality of entire trajectories

01:10:08.470 --> 01:10:10.430
because we could just score.

01:10:10.430 --> 01:10:12.610
We have a rollout
in our data set.

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:16.350
We might just be summing
up the reward accumulated

01:10:16.350 --> 01:10:17.870
over this trajectory.

01:10:17.870 --> 01:10:19.950
And then we could
basically filter out

01:10:19.950 --> 01:10:24.230
our data set such to only
keep the trajectories that

01:10:24.230 --> 01:10:29.230
are better than some
value, r bar, that we fix.

01:10:29.230 --> 01:10:33.590
So here is a very
simple example in which

01:10:33.590 --> 01:10:35.410
we know that there is
performance in place.

01:10:35.410 --> 01:10:39.550
Let us just focus ourselves on
the good ones-- on the ones that

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:42.110
are exceeding some threshold.

01:10:42.110 --> 01:10:44.410
Another mechanism could
be to do weighting.

01:10:44.410 --> 01:10:46.710
So we have, again,
some mechanism

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:52.580
of scoring our transitions and
trajectories through our reward.

01:10:52.580 --> 01:10:54.940
So let us modify the
learning problem.

01:10:54.940 --> 01:10:57.280
For example, the
maximum likelihood

01:10:57.280 --> 01:11:02.120
estimation objective such that
it becomes a weighted version

01:11:02.120 --> 01:11:05.200
of the original objective
where the weights come

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:07.480
from the performance itself.

01:11:07.480 --> 01:11:11.340
So we don't want to mimic
all trajectories blindly.

01:11:11.340 --> 01:11:14.240
So maximize the probability
of all trajectories blindly.

01:11:14.240 --> 01:11:16.360
But what we want
to do is maximize

01:11:16.360 --> 01:11:19.680
the weighted probability where
the weight is essentially

01:11:19.680 --> 01:11:21.600
the performance.

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:22.923
Yes.

01:11:22.923 --> 01:11:26.420
[INAUDIBLE] distinguish this
process of filtering the quote

01:11:26.420 --> 01:11:28.920
unquote, "best trajectories"
from what we were talking about

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:32.200
before, where we want to
augment purposefully all those

01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:34.360
suboptimal trainings?

01:11:34.360 --> 01:11:36.260
Yeah, it's a very good question.

01:11:36.260 --> 01:11:40.420
And I would say that the answer
here is very case-dependent.

01:11:40.420 --> 01:11:43.600
So it will really depend
on the application

01:11:43.600 --> 01:11:45.340
that you're working with.

01:11:48.040 --> 01:11:54.080
I would say that these filtering
techniques are particularly

01:11:54.080 --> 01:11:58.600
good in the case where you
have a very narrow task

01:11:58.600 --> 01:12:00.360
and where you don't
need to collect

01:12:00.360 --> 01:12:02.100
this corrective behavior.

01:12:02.100 --> 01:12:05.905
But well, here what you care
about is really focusing on I

01:12:05.905 --> 01:12:07.280
have these very
good trajectories

01:12:07.280 --> 01:12:10.240
that I want to learn
to exploit them.

01:12:10.240 --> 01:12:12.640
This could also be
very good if your data

01:12:12.640 --> 01:12:14.800
set quality is just very broad.

01:12:14.800 --> 01:12:16.880
So maybe I just
have trajectories

01:12:16.880 --> 01:12:18.880
that are extremely bad in here.

01:12:18.880 --> 01:12:22.320
And maybe my data set
is also extremely large.

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:26.520
And so you have no ways
of manually annotating

01:12:26.520 --> 01:12:28.100
what is useful and what is not.

01:12:28.100 --> 01:12:30.000
And so you devise
some simple strategy

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:33.800
to filter out bad things.

01:12:33.800 --> 01:12:37.920
So I would say it's
kind of parallel axis

01:12:37.920 --> 01:12:41.020
in devising towards
your solution.

01:12:45.890 --> 01:12:50.170
OK, and another idea
is that we might

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:53.370
want to enrich the
kind of conditioning

01:12:53.370 --> 01:12:56.090
that we have in our policy.

01:12:56.090 --> 01:12:59.370
So until now, the policy
for us was distribution

01:12:59.370 --> 01:13:03.610
over controls given states.

01:13:03.610 --> 01:13:07.730
But I think two particularly
popular approaches

01:13:07.730 --> 01:13:11.730
are to condition the policy
on maybe a candidate goal

01:13:11.730 --> 01:13:15.330
state for goal
condition policies

01:13:15.330 --> 01:13:17.690
or a candidate
performance that we

01:13:17.690 --> 01:13:19.470
wish we'd have from our model.

01:13:19.470 --> 01:13:22.850
So something where we might
condition it on a candidate

01:13:22.850 --> 01:13:26.090
total reward for our trajectory.

01:13:26.090 --> 01:13:29.410
Why are these
reformulations useful?

01:13:29.410 --> 01:13:31.130
And I think this can
be easily observed

01:13:31.130 --> 01:13:33.810
from the goal conditioned one.

01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:44.340
So say that we
have some data set

01:13:44.340 --> 01:13:47.980
where we have a
number of trajectories

01:13:47.980 --> 01:13:52.140
that go from some x1
to some candidate, xt.

01:13:52.140 --> 01:13:55.300
Now, maybe from the
perspective of the task,

01:13:55.300 --> 01:13:58.060
this trajectory is very
bad because maybe xt

01:13:58.060 --> 01:14:00.460
is very different
from the actual goal

01:14:00.460 --> 01:14:02.780
that we had over here.

01:14:02.780 --> 01:14:06.300
However, this is
a good trajectory

01:14:06.300 --> 01:14:11.900
for showing how we are
reaching maybe state, xi.

01:14:11.900 --> 01:14:15.420
So if the goal is not to solve
a task but to reach a state,

01:14:15.420 --> 01:14:18.380
then every trajectory
is a valid trajectory

01:14:18.380 --> 01:14:23.540
for showing an example of how we
are reaching a candidate goal.

01:14:23.540 --> 01:14:26.600
So, in our case,
this trajectory here,

01:14:26.600 --> 01:14:31.300
despite being bad for the task
of reaching this goal state,

01:14:31.300 --> 01:14:35.180
learning from this trajectory
as a means of having

01:14:35.180 --> 01:14:38.120
a general trajectory that
could reach any candidate goal

01:14:38.120 --> 01:14:40.670
this is actually a
good thing to do.

01:14:40.670 --> 01:14:45.150
So in some way, once we
have learned this goal

01:14:45.150 --> 01:14:49.810
reaching policy, then the
problem becomes at inference,

01:14:49.810 --> 01:14:52.510
I want to query
my policy to reach

01:14:52.510 --> 01:14:54.670
a state that is good for me.

01:14:54.670 --> 01:14:57.950
So I'm basically learning
a general mechanism

01:14:57.950 --> 01:15:02.790
to reach any candidate goal
point goal state from my state.

01:15:02.790 --> 01:15:05.390
And then at test time,
I want to condition

01:15:05.390 --> 01:15:11.030
this policy on reaching a
state that is good for me.

01:15:11.030 --> 01:15:12.710
But, in this case,
all trajectories

01:15:12.710 --> 01:15:16.470
are useful with this regard
because all trajectories

01:15:16.470 --> 01:15:19.510
are good realizations of
reaching all the states

01:15:19.510 --> 01:15:20.730
along that trajectory.

01:15:28.390 --> 01:15:30.970
And same thing for
reward conditioning.

01:15:34.390 --> 01:15:37.082
So just a quick mention on
inverse reinforcement learning

01:15:37.082 --> 01:15:38.540
more than I mentioned,
I would say,

01:15:38.540 --> 01:15:42.840
pointers for you to dive
deeper if you're interested.

01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:45.900
Remember that in
behavior cloning,

01:15:45.900 --> 01:15:49.560
we wish to estimate the policy
that approximates or mimics

01:15:49.560 --> 01:15:51.120
the behavior of an expert.

01:15:51.120 --> 01:15:52.860
In inverse
reinforcement learning

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:54.800
we care about learning
the objective that is

01:15:54.800 --> 01:15:58.440
being optimized by the expert.

01:15:58.440 --> 01:16:01.023
And, in general, I would
say that-- so more formally,

01:16:01.023 --> 01:16:02.440
the goal of
reinforcement learning

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:04.400
is to recover the
reward function, which

01:16:04.400 --> 01:16:08.360
we think is being
optimized by the expert.

01:16:08.360 --> 01:16:12.000
In practice, this means that
we will be often parameterizing

01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:15.520
our reward by some
parameters, w,

01:16:15.520 --> 01:16:19.040
and the goal is to find the
values of those parameters that

01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:22.400
better explain the
expert demonstrations.

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:26.160
There are a number of inverse
reinforcement learning

01:16:26.160 --> 01:16:27.820
algorithms, as you can imagine.

01:16:27.820 --> 01:16:29.400
But I would say
that a high level

01:16:29.400 --> 01:16:31.640
the skeleton of most
of these algorithms

01:16:31.640 --> 01:16:33.480
is something like
the pseudocode here

01:16:33.480 --> 01:16:37.770
on the slide, whereby we will
start from a data set of expert

01:16:37.770 --> 01:16:41.370
demonstrations, and we will
have some initialized reward

01:16:41.370 --> 01:16:46.090
function parameters, w, and
policy parameters, theta.

01:16:46.090 --> 01:16:47.970
And then most
algorithms typically

01:16:47.970 --> 01:16:50.870
alternate between two
separate processes.

01:16:50.870 --> 01:16:53.170
We'll have a first
process whereby

01:16:53.170 --> 01:16:56.170
we update the reward
function parameters according

01:16:56.170 --> 01:16:58.010
to some objective.

01:16:58.010 --> 01:17:02.890
And then we will use these
newly computed parameters, w,

01:17:02.890 --> 01:17:05.650
to optimize for the
parameters of the policy.

01:17:05.650 --> 01:17:07.770
So we will have
an estimate of w.

01:17:07.770 --> 01:17:11.050
We will use this estimate to
basically maximize the policy

01:17:11.050 --> 01:17:14.210
with respect to that objective.

01:17:14.210 --> 01:17:17.490
And then use this
policy to increase,

01:17:17.490 --> 01:17:21.570
to improve the estimate of w.

01:17:21.570 --> 01:17:23.640
Again, so this is very
high level discussion.

01:17:23.640 --> 01:17:25.890
But just to let you know
that most of these algorithms

01:17:25.890 --> 01:17:29.530
should be seen from this lens.

01:17:29.530 --> 01:17:33.410
And popular algorithms are
apprenticeship learning,

01:17:33.410 --> 01:17:36.350
maximum margin planning,
and maximum entropy IRL.

01:17:36.350 --> 01:17:38.650
So we definitely
start from those.

01:17:38.650 --> 01:17:42.410
And these algorithms
are, to some extent,

01:17:42.410 --> 01:17:46.810
all try to address the challenge
or one of the key challenges

01:17:46.810 --> 01:17:49.290
for inverse reinforcement
learning, which

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:51.810
is one of reward ambiguity.

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:53.330
And this is
basically the problem

01:17:53.330 --> 01:17:57.290
where there could be many
reward functions that

01:17:57.290 --> 01:17:58.850
explain the same data.

01:17:58.850 --> 01:18:02.130
And so these
mechanisms really try

01:18:02.130 --> 01:18:04.890
to design these two
individual processes

01:18:04.890 --> 01:18:09.030
such that we don't fall
in this reward ambiguity.

01:18:14.838 --> 01:18:20.290
So, just to conclude, we have
been introducing, I would say,

01:18:20.290 --> 01:18:23.370
most of the techniques
to basically build

01:18:23.370 --> 01:18:25.170
a mental model, I would
say, of how to deal

01:18:25.170 --> 01:18:26.470
with imitation learning.

01:18:26.470 --> 01:18:29.410
We focus most of our
narrative on behavior cloning

01:18:29.410 --> 01:18:34.780
where the v0 kind of naive
formalism of behavior cloning

01:18:34.780 --> 01:18:37.360
is that we have a data set
of expert demonstrations,

01:18:37.360 --> 01:18:41.260
and we want to learn a
mapping that approximates

01:18:41.260 --> 01:18:43.580
the behaviors in our data.

01:18:43.580 --> 01:18:48.180
We saw how this naive version
is basically prone to suffering

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:50.780
from two main
challenges, compounding

01:18:50.780 --> 01:18:54.780
errors and the multi-modality
of behavior data.

01:18:54.780 --> 01:18:56.380
And we saw how we
could tackle this

01:18:56.380 --> 01:18:59.340
from a perspective
of data collection

01:18:59.340 --> 01:19:02.640
through smart augmentations,
a perspective of algorithms,

01:19:02.640 --> 01:19:04.860
say DAgger, and
expressive models that

01:19:04.860 --> 01:19:07.380
try to capture the
multi-modality of the data

01:19:07.380 --> 01:19:10.060
itself and then
briefly mentioned

01:19:10.060 --> 01:19:13.980
two alternative formulations,
such as reinforcement learning

01:19:13.980 --> 01:19:17.320
through via supervised
learning and inverse RL.

01:19:21.140 --> 01:19:24.500
Any questions on this?

01:19:24.500 --> 01:19:27.350
All right, then see
you on Wednesday.

